// A `for` whose body returns does NOT return on every path, and until v0.0.242 the checker thought it
// did — with the worst possible consequence.
//
// `for x in xs { return x; }` over an EMPTY array runs zero times, so the path that skips the loop
// falls out of the function with no `return`. `stmt_returns` answered `block_returns(body)` for `For`,
// so this program typechecked and then:
//
//     error: LLVM module verification failed:
//     Basic Block in function 'bx.f' does not have terminator!
//
// **The checker said yes and LLVM said no, so the user is told the COMPILER is broken rather than that
// their program is missing a `return`.** A refusal that names the wrong party is worse than a wrong
// answer, because there is nothing the reader can act on.
//
// `while` had always answered `false` here for exactly this reason, and `stmt_diverges` two functions
// away already said the true thing about the same construct. Two rules disagreed and nothing compared
// them.
//
// Found by a subagent implementing ranges: it did not want to write `false` for one loop form and
// `true` for the other, so it measured which was right. **A question about new work auditing old work
// is the cheapest audit there is.**
function f(xs: [Int]) -> Int {
    for x in xs {
        return x;
    }
}
print(f([1, 2]));
